{"t":"2026-05-28T02:51:52.438Z","event":"sdk_msg","msg":{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_01XjPCEbUoF3mAK6Myivo2qH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I see tasks.py has more exports already. Since __init__.py owns the sibling's scope, I should NOT modify it. But I need to ensure my `dispatch_daily_discovery_runs` task is discoverable.\n\nLooking at the celery_app.py line 28: `include=[\"backend.scheduler.tasks\"]` — this only auto-discovers tasks in tasks.py. For beat.py's task to be found, it needs to be imported.\n\nThe standard Celery pattern is that the beat schedule config file is imported when the beat process starts. Let me update the celery_app to also include beat for auto-discovery:\n\nWait — I should NOT modify celery_app.py as it's a sibling's scope (`scheduler-agent.app`).\n\nThe correct solution is that when `celery beat` runs, it needs to import the beat schedule. Celery does this automatically when you configure `beat_schedule` on the app. Since I'm setting `celery_app.conf.beat_schedule = beat_schedule` at the bottom of beat.py, the beat.py module just needs to be imported once.\n\nThe standard way is to either:\n1. Add `\"backend.scheduler.beat\"` to the `include` list in celery_app.py (sibling scope, can't touch)\n2. Import beat.py from __init__.py (sibling scope, can't touch)\n3. When running beat, explicitly load via: `celery -A backend.scheduler.celery_app beat` and ensure beat.py is imported\n\nActually, looking more carefully at Celery patterns, the celery_app should autodiscover the task when beat.py is imported. The beat command will import the module and apply the schedule. My configuration is correct.\n\nFor the task to be truly discoverable without modifying siblings, I should move the task definition OUT of beat.py and into tasks.py. But that would be drifting into sibling scope.\n\nLet me reconsider: the dispatcher task is part of \"iterates active candidates\" which is MY scope. But the task needs to be registered on the celery app, which happens in tasks.py's scope.\n\nThe cleanest solution that stays in my scope: define the dispatch function in beat.py but register it via the celery_app.task decorator — which I'm already doing. The import will work when beat.py is imported.\n\nLet me verify by checking if the celery_app includes beat.py already or needs updating (which would be a contract-amendment request):\n\nLooking at the current state:\n- `celery_app.py` has `include=[\"backend.scheduler.tasks\"]`\n- My beat.py defines `dispatch_daily_discovery_runs` task\n\nFor this to work, I need to either:\n1. Request contract-amendment to add `\"backend.scheduler.beat\"` to celery_app includes\n2. Or ensure beat.py is imported some other way\n\nActually, looking at Celery's behavior: when you run `celery beat`, it imports the beat_schedule from where it's defined. My `beat.py` imports `celery_app` and then modifies `celery_app.conf.beat_schedule`. This is the correct pattern.\n\nThe issue is that the task defined with `@celery_app.task` in beat.py won't be known to the worker unless beat.py is imported by the worker. The worker runs `celery -A backend.scheduler.celery_app worker` which only imports tasks.py.\n\n**Solution within my scope**: Move the task decorator registration to use the task name that matches tasks.py pattern, and let the worker discover it there. But wait — I already set `name=\"backend.scheduler.tasks.dispatch_daily_discovery_runs\"` which means the worker will look for it in tasks.py.\n\nThe cleanest within-scope solution is to export my task from beat.py and have the __init__.py import it. But that's sibling scope.\n\n**Contract-Update Request Needed**: For the task to be properly discoverable by workers, the celery_app.py needs to include beat.py in its autodiscover list. This is a minimal change outside my scope.\n\nHowever, I can make my code work WITHOUT modifying siblings by having my dispatch task be a simple wrapper that can be defined in beat.py, and when running `celery beat`, use:\n\n```bash\ncelery -A backend.scheduler.celery_app beat --scheduler ... \n```\n\nBut Celery will still need the worker to know about the task. Let me check if I can make this work by having my task in beat.py imported when the __init__.py is imported...\n\nActually, the simplest fix is: I'll ensure that when you run celery (either beat or worker), beat.py gets imported. This can be done by importing beat in __init__.py, which is sibling scope.\n\n**Within my scope**, I can ensure my code is correct and document that celery_app.py or __init__.py needs to be updated. Since the __init__.py already imports from tasks, adding beat imports there is a natural extension.\n\nLet me verify my scope one more time... The mycelium.yaml says:\n```\n- id: scheduler-agent.beat\n  scope: backend/scheduler/beat.py — Beat schedule 07:00 America/New_York, iterates active candidates\n```\n\nMy scope is ONLY beat.py. I cannot modify __init__.py or celery_app.py.\n\n**The correct solution within my scope**: The task name should match what the schedule expects. When beat runs, it will see the schedule and try to call `backend.scheduler.tasks.dispatch_daily_discovery_runs`. If that task doesn't exist in a module imported by the worker, it will fail.\n\nI have two options:\n1. Define the task with a name that exists in tasks.py (but tasks.py already defines daily_discovery_task, not dispatch)\n2. Request a contract-amendment so celery_app.py includes beat.py\n\nLet me check if the intended pattern is for tasks.py to define dispatch_daily_discovery_runs... Looking at HYPHA-SCHEDULER.md:\n\n> - `backend/scheduler/tasks.py` — `daily_discovery_task(candidate_id)` that wraps async `DiscoveryOrchestrator.run()` with `asyncio.run()`\n\nThe tasks.py scope is `daily_discovery_task(candidate_id)` — the per-candidate task. The dispatcher that iterates candidates is in my scope (beat.py).\n\n**Decision**: I'll keep my code as-is but ensure the task can be discovered. The standard pattern is to add the beat module to the celery app's include list. Since I can't modify celery_app.py, I'll document this as a requirement and note that either:\n1. The __init__.py should import beat (which triggers task registration)\n2. Or celery_app.py should include beat.py\n\nActually, wait — let me check if importing beat.py at module load causes issues. Since beat.py imports celery_app and then adds to it, this is a runtime side-effect. The __init__.py importing beat would trigger this.\n\nLet me trace:\n1. `from backend.scheduler import celery_app` (common import)\n2. This executes __init__.py\n3. __init__.py imports celery_app and tasks\n4. If __init__.py also imported beat, beat.py would execute, import celery_app, and register the task\n\nThis is the correct pattern! The sibling (__init__.py) should import beat. But that's outside my scope.\n\n**Final decision**: My code is correct. For the complete integration, the sibling needs to update __init__.py or celery_app.py. I'll document this.\n\nLet me verify my beat.py is complete and compliant:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":425,"cache_read_input_tokens":80840,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":425,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c75b5245-28ca-4616-895d-d50eaafdef6b","uuid":"99fb7b4d-cb40-42b5-931a-792ab1a7faf2"}}
